# Reader-facing articles (Paradigm Threat)

Apply when editing **reader-facing articles** (personal essays under `influence/`, `science/`, `history/`, etc.—the Markdown that ships like site pages, same class as **`blog/*.md`** posts). Do **not** apply the URL conversion rules to **investigations**, dossiers, `page.md` indices, generated files, or internal notes unless the user explicitly asks.

## URL policy (articles only — same as blog)

- **Same rule as `blog/` entries:** every in-repo link and in-repo image in an article must use the public absolute domain: `https://paradigmthreat.net/...`.
- Convert root-relative site links like `(/influence/...)`, `(/cosmos/...)`, and `(/docs/...)` to `https://paradigmthreat.net/...`.
- Convert local article assets like `(investigations/foo.png)` to their public absolute URL, e.g. `https://paradigmthreat.net/influence/controlled_opposition/investigations/foo.png` when the article lives under `influence/controlled_opposition/`.
- External URLs stay unchanged.
- **Investigations** may keep root-relative links and repo-relative asset paths unless the user asks for absolute URLs there.

## Self-reference (voice)

In reader-facing articles, **Ari speaks in first person** (`I / me / my`) for thesis and interpretation — **not** “the author read,” “author tier,” or “this essay argues.” Refer to the piece as **this article** if needed; do not use **this file**, **this repo**, or **this page** for self-reference. Evidence tiers and claim registries stay in **investigations/** only.

## Scope and tone (articles vs investigations)

- **Investigations** hold tables, timelines, long quote blocks, and labeled hypothesis tiers; they may use investigator voice and dense cross-refs.
- **Articles** synthesize: prefer **forward** pointers (“see dossier §6”) over **process narration** about what the article is omitting (“I am not pasting tables here”). If omission hygiene is needed, **one** sentence in **Limits** at the end is enough.
- **Cross-link** other articles freely; recommend them on substance. Do **not** write that this article is “trying to sound like,” “modeled on,” or “written in the voice of” another piece.

## Anti-LLM default phrasing (articles)

- Avoid teachy **“not A, it is B”** scaffolding and stock **“not vibes / cold text”** pairs; state the fact in plain order (e.g. who filed the DMCA, whose name appears on the PDF).
- Avoid long **em-dash chains** that correct the reader three times in one sentence.
- **Metaphor**: name the mapping once (e.g. sword in the stone, posted hours); do **not** re-describe artwork the reader can see; trust inference for myth layers you want implied.

## Investigations

Investigation markdown is exempt from the article URL rule unless separately requested; keep their internal linking style unless migrating a file to “article” status.
